[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What value gets stored as the identity? → Record, id = username (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — [38;5;153m{ id, username }[39m it is, with consumers reading [38;5;153m.id[39m.

  Next: [1mhow long should it persist?[0m You said it should persist, but that word spans three quite different lifetimes, and
  this one has a privacy consequence rather than just a code one.

  The relevant constraint is something we already decided: attribution-only means there's [1mno logout[0m in scope. Nothing in
  the app will ever clear this. So whatever lifetime I pick is the lifetime, with no escape hatch for the user.

  - [1mSession storage[0m — survives page reloads and navigation between pages in the tab; clears when the tab closes. The
    stored username lives exactly as long as the person is using the app.
  - [1mLocal storage[0m — survives browser restart, persists until something explicitly deletes it. Buys "still logged in
    tomorrow." Costs: with no logout, the last person's username sits in the browser indefinitely, and the next person
    on a shared machine gets their submissions stamped with it.
  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a module variable. Clears on every reload, which I don't think meets "it should persist," but it's
    the zero-risk option if reloads are rare.

  [1mI'd recommend session storage.[0m It's the lifetime that matches an app with no logout: the identity goes away when the
  user does. Local storage is the one to pick only if you specifically want the identity to outlive the browser session
  — and if you do, I'd want to add a way to clear it, which nudges us back toward the access-state design you ruled out.
